A||||7||GT Bastard||Anybody use the stabilizer bearing/seal that the Fierostore sells?||||5||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||06-27-2001||11:41 PM||||Part #:55125 <P>I've had problems with axle seal leaks and this looks like a good solution.  Anybody have experience?<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/gtbastard.gif">     <P>"My heroes are John Wayne, the Simpsons, and whoever canceled Full House/Bob Saget."||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||06-28-2001||12:15 AM||||if your existing seals are leaking one of three things has happened.<P>1. the seal is bad<P>2. the bearing is bad.<P>3. a new seal was installed into a damaged transmission. It's all too comon for the seal opening in the tranny to get gored while removing a bad seal. All replacemetn seals will leak unless they are installed with something to fill the gap in the Al tranny case. (Big leaks may not ever seal.)<P>to fix...<P>1. obviously you replace the seal.<P>2. you replace the bearing and seal. Don't count on an addon bearing to fix this. The addon is intended to reduce wear. not make up for bad bearings.<P>3. the usualy fix is to coat the seal opening with Permatex Super 300 or Balkamp aviation sealer. (These won't make boogers inside the tranny.) If the scratch in the Al is big then you might have to try using RTV silicon to seal it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Terrybogin||06-28-2001||12:35 AM||||If you decide that you still want the bearing/seals- Rodney used to have them for half the Fiero Store's price.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodney||06-28-2001||08:31 AM||||I have them for the same price I believe. But my shipping is always less. I also sell the Locktite/sealer. I have seen several people in the past put them in dry and have had oil leakage problems. So I include a write up with installation tips which stresses that you use a sealant to prevent oil leakage.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodney||06-28-2001||08:35 AM||||2. you replace the bearing and seal. Don't count on an addon bearing to fix this. The addon is intended to reduce wear. not make up for bad bearings.<P>I have sold a few sets to people with bad axle bearings. They knew their axle bearings were bad. They were just trying to get some more miles out of their transmissions before they had to pull them for rebuild. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fiero_24||06-28-2001||08:57 AM||||I have added them to an 86 Sport coupe, and they worked great.  I had a lot of axle vibration and it solved the problem.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||GT Bastard||06-28-2001||10:21 AM||||If I remember correctly, the outer ring of the seals are fine.  It's the inside surface that's the problem.  Both of them leak, so I was thinking that possibly the axles were scored-up when they were installed.  You guy are talking about just putting permatex around the outer edge of the axle seals right?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Steve Normington||06-28-2001||12:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Rodney:<BR><B>So I include a write up with installation tips which stresses that you use a sealant to prevent oil leakage.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I installed them without sealant and they are leaking. Is there an easy way to remove the bearings? I've tried tapping a chisel into the joint between the bearing and the tranny case and it won't budge.||||reg||1
